    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Relapsing Plasmodium vivax malaria (B51.9)</content>. The presenting fevers now have their explanation. Three attacks of fever with chills, each months after leaving an area where P. vivax predominates, after blood-stage treatment alone, and P. vivax DNA in blood between attacks, fit relapses from dormant liver stages. Hemoglobin 13.0 g/dL and platelets 141 x10^3/uL are slightly low, consistent with recent attacks. G6PD activity 11.8 U/g Hb is normal, so primaquine is safe. Dr. Okonkwo-Hale recommends chloroquine for the blood stage, because P. vivax from the Central American coast is chloroquine-sensitive, with primaquine for radical cure. [S]<list><item>Chloroquine 1,000 mg now, then 500 mg at 6, 24 and 48 hours.</item><item>Primaquine 30 mg base daily with food for 14 days, starting today.</item><item>Report dark urine, breathlessness or blue lips at once.</item><item>Blood smear and PCR a week after finishing primaquine, and a blood count.</item><item>Review in four weeks.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Pre-travel care for the deployment (Z71.84)</content>. Dr. Okonkwo-Hale has drawn up the pre-travel plan: yellow fever vaccine (required for French Guiana), hepatitis A, hepatitis B if not immune, typhoid, malaria chemoprophylaxis, protection against mosquitoes and other vectors, food and water precautions, and terminal prophylaxis on return. [S]<list><item>Hepatitis B status from the preventive visit screening.</item><item>Vaccines at a pre-travel visit in the summer, well before departure.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Malaria diagnosis: smears, rapid antigen test and species PCR</td><td>At diagnosis</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Smears and rapid antigen test negative at intake; PCR from the intake sample detected P. vivax DNA</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Quantitative G6PD activity before primaquine or tafenoquine</td><td>Once, before an 8-aminoquinoline</td><td>Completed today</td><td>11.8 U/g Hb at intake, normal</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Blood count and bilirubin for hemolysis on primaquine</td><td>Baseline, then after the course</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Hemoglobin 13.0 g/dL, platelets 141 x10^3/uL and bilirubin 1.1 mg/dL at intake; repeat after primaquine</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Infectious diseases (travel medicine) consultation</td><td>Once</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen by Dr. Okonkwo-Hale today</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Adverse effects: nausea, abdominal pain, dark urine, breathlessness, cyanosis</td><td>During the course</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Counseled before the first doses; take with food</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Relapse surveillance and the fever action plan</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Fever card given: a smear the same day for any fever</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Follow-up blood smear after treatment (ideally within one week of finishing)</td><td>About one week after finishing</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Smear and PCR ordered for a week after the last primaquine dose</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>No further malaria relapse in the six months after radical cure. In progress; target 1705-10-13 [S]</item><item>All required and recommended travel vaccines given, with a valid yellow fever certificate, at least ten days before departure. In progress; target 1705-10-13 [S]</item><item>Knows to have a malaria test the same day for any fever, during travel and for months after return. In progress; target 1705-03-31 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Treatment of Uncomplicated Malaria (clinical guidance) (2026); CDC Yellow Book: Health Information for International Travel, 2026 edition. Malaria (2026); The practice of travel medicine: guidelines by the Infectious Diseases Society of America (Hill DR, et al. Clin Infect Dis 2006;43(12):1499-1539) (2006)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Review in four weeks. Next scheduled visit 1705-03-31: six-week review after chloroquine and primaquine: blood count, adverse effects, relapse surveillance. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
